\ ===================== MACHINE/MODEL DEPENDENT SECTION ======================
\ Native code, TOS in register.
\ r13/sp is data stack, r14/lr is used for links.
\ TOS in r5, RSP in r6, UP in r7.
\ TODO: If that starts getting tight on Thumb-friendly registers, UP could be
\ moved to a high register fairly harmlessly.
HOST DEFINITIONS

\ These are not sensible to call when doing native compilation and with separate
\ data spaces.
: HERE ( -- ) 99 throw ;
: DP   ( -- ) 98 throw ;

\ First line of code, the entry point. This is at $08000000, the start of ROM.
?stack-clear
code-space @ b,   \ A dummy jump for now that will be adjusted later.
?stack-clear

\
\ Internal helper that compiles a return into a word. This is called by the
\ inliner for early, explicit `EXIT` on native code platforms.
\ Not technically part of the model interface with the metacompiler.
: return, ( -- )
  +return ;

\ Actually **ends** a nonprimitive definition. See `return,` above for an early
\ explicit exit. This buffers the end node and also triggers native compilation.
: EXIT,  ( -- ) return, done-comp ;

\ DOES> design for buffered compilation ======================================
\ This is different from the original TC-Forth DODOES scheme.
\ Sequence 1: while eg. : CONSTANT create , DOES> @ ; is being defined.
\ Sequence 2: while CONSTANT is running, defining a new constant.
\ Sequence 3: while a particular constant is executing.

\ DOES> is an immediate word, it runs during "Sequence 1".
\ It compiles a dodoes node and an end node at the end of e.g. `CONSTANT`.
\ Then the Forth code after `DOES>` is compiled, as a new nonprimitive.

\ Sequence 2 (eg. `7 CONSTANT FOO`) `CREATE`s the new header with the default
\ `DOVAR` code, but then the code compiled by the `dodoes` node replaces it with
\ a call to the second, `DOES>` nonprimitive.

\ Sequence 3 executes `FOO` like any other word. Its code has been modified to
\ jump to the `DOES>` nonprimitive, which has a preamble that fetches the data
\ field.

\ Called during the `HOST ACTS:` for a `DOES>`, during Phase 1, i.e. compilation
\ of a defining word like `CONSTANT`. In general, it must do four things:
\ 1. Close the current definition, like `;` would.
\ 2. Open a new definition and capture its xt.
\ 3. Compile any preamble that the target `DOES>` needs.
\ 4. Return the target-xt for the dodoes-code.

\ Thumb-specific: The code calling e.g. a `VALUE` does a `BL some-val`.
\ That puts the return address into `lr`, but don't forget that it's actually
\ `($ + 4) OR 1`, where `$` is the address of (first opcode of) the `BL`
\ instruction itself.
\ So that's the right pointer, except for the extra 1 on it.
: DOES, ( -- xt-does )
  compiling @ IF done-comp THEN
  code-space @ \ Capture the xt of the does code
  begin-comp +dodoes ;


\ Opens a nonprimitive definition, compiling any boilerplate that it needs.
\ For threaded code, either the address of DOCOL/ENTER or a jump to it.
\ For native code, any preamble that's required to handle the return address.
\ Begin compilation, then append a "starter" node as the first entry.
\ For Thumb, the return address is in `lr`, and we always save it to the return
\ stack for safety, for now. But that's the job of `assemble-starter`; see the
\ gba/buffer-impl.ft.
: ,docol ( -- ) begin-comp   +starter ;


\ CREATEd words and DOES> ====================================================
\ These words need to push their own PFA to the data stack and allow for a
\ later `!dodoes` that makes them call the `DOES>` code without consuming any
\ extra space.
\ The final, post-`!dodoes` form looks like this:
\ $00: PUSH {tos}
\ $02: LDR tos, [pc, #2]   $+4 = $06; +2 cells = $0d; AND NOT 2 = $0c = &PFA
\ $04: LDR r0,  [pc, #0]   $+4 = $08; +0 cells = $08; AND NOT 2 = $08 = &dodoes-xt
\ $06: BX r0               Jump to the dodoes-xt
\ $08: dodoes-xt
\ $0a: ...
\ $0c: PFA
\ $0e: ...
\
\ So a basic ,dovar compiles the following:
\ $00: PUSH {tos}
\ $02: LDR tos, [pc, #2]   $+4 = $06; +2 cells = $0d; AND NOT 2 = $0c = &PFA
\ $04: BX lr               Returning directly to the caller.
\ $06: (blank)
\ $08: (blank)
\ $0a: (blank)
\ $0c: PFA
\ $0e: ...
\ NOTE: The above PC-relative arithmetic only works if the xt of this code is
\ word-aligned! So ,dovar checks that, and all xts should be word-aligned, even
\ though Thumb could support halfword alignment.
: ,dovar ( -- )
  code-space @ 3 and ABORT" ,dovar called with unaligned address"

  thumb
  {{ tos }}      push,
  2 # pc [+] tos ldr,
  lr             bx,

  3 0 DO 0 asm-half, LOOP
  \ That leaves the code-space pointer at xt+$0c, which is exactly where the
  \ following shared host code's call to `t,pfa` will put the PFA.
;

\ Called from `HOST (DOES>)` to overwrite the code in the word at word-xt,
\ which was compiled by `,dovar` above, so that it calls the `;CODE` or `DOES>`
\ at `does-xt` instead.
\ See above for what the final code looks like.
: !dodoes ( does-xt word-xt -- )
  code-space @ >R                     \ Save the current location
  1 invert and   4 + code-space !     \ Move to the `BX lr` slot ( does-xt )
  thumb
  0 # pc [+] r0 ldr,
  r0            bx,                   ( does-xt )
  1 or asm-word,                      \ Making sure it's Thumb-safe!
  R> code-space ! ;


\ No inline strings allowed - store them in code space with `STRING`.
: ,dostring ( c-addr u -- ) 97 throw ;

\ For compiled Thumb the parameter field is always at xt+$c; see `,dovar`.
: t>pfa ( t-xt -- pf-slot ) 1 invert and   $c + ;


\ For GBA, the tasks start at the top of IWRAM.
\ Entry point at 64KB, dictionary follows, and tasks start at 8MB.
$00800000 CONSTANT task-area0

\ For the main task, 64 cells of return stack and 128 cells of data stack.
64  tcells CONSTANT default/rs
128 tcells CONSTANT default/ds

tcode-align
label entry-point 0 asm-word,

\ Compiles a jump to `entry-point` at the end of `kmain`.
\ NOTE: This is **ARM code**, and this BX is the transition to Thumb.
: >entry-point, ( -- )
  arm
  entry-point r0 ldr-pc,
  r0 bx, ;
